---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Container from '../components/Container.astro';
import AdvocacyBuilder from '../components/AdvocacyBuilder';
import cornerSign from '../assets/photos/andrei-shiptenko-FzYbSqjzmqM-unsplash.jpg';
---

<Base
  title="Your advocacy sheet"
  description="Pick what you want your kid's school to change about phones, devices, and AI, and get a one-page handout with your requests, the evidence, and the script. Free, no sign-up."
>
  <header class="no-print border-b border-line bg-surface-2 py-14 sm:py-20">
    <Container>
      <div class="grid items-center gap-x-12 gap-y-10 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.15fr)_minmax(0,0.85fr)]">
      <div>
        <p class="mb-3.5 font-label text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-primary-text">
          For talking to schools
        </p>
        <h1 class="font-display text-[clamp(38px,4vw,52px)] font-extrabold leading-[1.04] tracking-[-0.015em] text-navy">
          Your advocacy sheet
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-ink-soft">
          No two parents want the same thing from a school. Some want phones gone and the laptops
          kept, some want AI off and everything else on, some want the applications to remain installed but used sparingly or with guardrails. Set the levers to what you want, and get a one-page handout with your requests,
          the evidence for each, and the script for the person you are talking to.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-[18px] max-w-[40em] text-[0.97rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          <span class="font-semibold text-primary-text">No sign-up, and no data leaves your browser.</span>{' '}
          The sheet is assembled on your device, and printing it or saving it as a PDF happens
          there too. Nothing you enter is sent to us or to anyone else.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-[240px_240px_24px_24px] bg-surface-3">
        <Image
          src={cornerSign}
          alt="On the granite corner of a building, a black arrow sign reads: a change may be just around the corner."
          widths={[520, 1040]}
          sizes="(min-width: 768px) 480px, 92vw"
          class="h-[400px] w-full object-cover object-[62%_50%]"
        />
      </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </header>

  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[900px] px-6 pb-24 pt-12 sm:px-10">
    <AdvocacyBuilder client:load />

    {/* The audit: static, so it reads and prints whether or not a sheet was
        built. It prints last, as a parent-facing page. */}
    <section id="audit" class="audit-page print-sheet mt-16 rounded-[28px] border-2 border-line bg-surface px-10 pb-9 pt-8">
      <p class="font-label text-[11.5px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent-text">
        For you &middot; before you ask for anything
      </p>
      <h2 class="mt-2.5 font-display text-[26px] font-extrabold leading-[1.15] text-navy">
        Eleven questions that show whether anyone is at the wheel
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[1.01rem] leading-[1.62] text-ink-soft">
        You cannot ask for the right change until you know what the school actually does, and
        many schools have never been asked. These questions are not accusations. A school that
        answers them easily is running things well, and you have learned to relax. A school that
        cannot answer them has learned something too.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-5">
        {[
          'Who administers the devices our kids use – a staff person, a contractor, or nobody in particular?',
          'Which AI features are currently enabled on student accounts, and did anyone review them before they were turned on?',
          'What sites can a student access in the browser during independent work time? Is there a blocklist, an allowlist, or nothing?',
          'Are shared devices wiped between students, or does the next kid inherit the last one’s history, cookies, and signed-in accounts?',
          'Can students sign in to personal accounts, like email or YouTube, on school devices?',
          'Are cameras and microphones off by default, and what turns them on?',
          'What data do the apps on these devices collect about our kids, and who read the privacy terms before adoption?',
          'Does the school network filter content, and when did anyone last check what students can actually reach through it?',
          'When a new tool or feature gets enabled, do parents hear about it before or after?',
          'Does the school run monitoring software that scans what students type, search, and view? If so, what gets flagged, who sees the flags, and does it keep watching on a device that goes home?',
          'If we asked for one setting to change tomorrow, who has the login to change it?',
        ].map((q, i) => (
          <li class="grid grid-cols-[30px_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-3 border-t border-line py-3.5">
            <span
              class="flex h-[26px] w-[26px] items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-panel-accent font-label text-[13.5px] font-bold text-navy"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              {i + 1}
            </span>
            <p class="text-[1.01rem] leading-[1.58] text-ink">{q}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 border-t border-line pt-4 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink-soft">
        The last one matters most. Every guardrail this page can ask for is a setting that already exists, and change will be made by whomever holds that login.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="no-print mt-14 border-t border-line pt-6 text-[0.97rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      The thinking behind every lever is on the{' '}
      <a href="/decisions/asking-the-school" class="font-semibold text-primary-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-navy">asking the school for less</a>{' '}
      page: who actually controls access, why the grantable asks come first, and why three families asking for change beat one who's perceived as complaining.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style is:global>
  @media print {
    /* The sheet is the only thing that prints, and it has to land on one
       page. Screen paddings above it collapse, type steps down, and the
       Tailwind rem sizes are overridden pointwise since a container
       font-size cannot reach them. */
    .advocacy-sheet-wrap {
      margin-top: 0 !important;
    }
    .mx-auto {
      padding-top: 0 !important;
      padding-bottom: 0 !important;
    }
    .print-sheet p,
    .print-sheet li {
      font-size: 10pt !important;
      line-height: 1.38 !important;
      break-inside: avoid;
    }
    .print-sheet h2 {
      font-size: 19pt !important;
      margin-top: 4pt !important;
    }
    .print-sheet h3 {
      font-size: 9pt !important;
      margin-top: 9pt !important;
    }
    .script-page,
    .howto-page {
      break-before: page;
    }
    /* The audit breaks onto its own page only when a built sheet precedes
       it; unconditional, it printed a blank first page on an unbuilt page. */
    .mx-auto:has(.advocacy-sheet-wrap) .audit-page {
      break-before: page;
    }
    .print-sheet .sheet-script {
      margin-top: 5pt !important;
      padding-top: 0 !important;
      padding-bottom: 0 !important;
    }
    .print-sheet ol li {
      padding-top: 3pt !important;
      padding-bottom: 3pt !important;
    }
    .print-sheet .sheet-sources,
    .print-sheet .sheet-sources p {
      font-size: 7pt !important;
      line-height: 1.4 !important;
    }
    .print-sheet .sheet-sources {
      margin-top: 10pt !important;
    }
  }
</style>
